<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing">
    <title>Pointer Variables and Dereferencing</title>

    <p>
        A pointer declaration includes the type of object that the pointer is
        expected to identify. The <c>*</c> in a declaration says that the new
        variable is a pointer.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_ch9_pointer_declaration_and_dereference"
             language="cpp" interactive="yes">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int score = 90;
    int* score_pointer = &score;

    std::cout << *score_pointer << '\n';

    *score_pointer = 95;
    std::cout << score << '\n';

    return 0;
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        In <c>int* score_pointer</c>, the <c>*</c> is part of the declaration
        syntax. It says that <c>score_pointer</c> has type pointer to
        <c>int</c>. In <c>*score_pointer</c>, the <c>*</c> is the dereference
        operator. It follows the stored address and accesses the object there.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table_ch9_pointer_symbols">
        <title>Two pointer-related operators</title>
        <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
            <col width="25%"/>
            <col width="37%"/>
            <col width="38%"/>
            <row header="yes">
                <cell>Expression</cell>
                <cell>Role</cell>
                <cell>Meaning</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><c>int* pointer</c></cell>
                <cell>Pointer declaration</cell>
                <cell>Declares <c>pointer</c> as a pointer to an <c>int</c>.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><c>&amp;score</c></cell>
                <cell>Address-of operator</cell>
                <cell>Produces the address of <c>score</c>.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><c>*pointer</c></cell>
                <cell>Dereference operator</cell>
                <cell>Accesses the object identified by <c>pointer</c>.</cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
        These declarations are equivalent:
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_ch9_pointer_declaration_spacing"
             language="cpp">
        <code><![CDATA[
int* first_pointer;
int *second_pointer;
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        In this course, we will declare one variable per line. This avoids a
        common source of confusion:
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_ch9_one_pointer_per_line"
             language="cpp">
        <code><![CDATA[
int* first_pointer;
int second_value;
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        In <c>int* first_pointer, second_value;</c>, only
        <c>first_pointer</c> is a pointer. The second variable is an ordinary
        <c>int</c>. One declaration per line makes this distinction visible.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_lvalues-and-rvalues">
        <title>Lvalues and Rvalues: From Assignment to Expression Categories</title>

        <p>
            The names <term>lvalue</term> and <term>rvalue</term> originally
            came from assignment expressions. An lvalue appeared on the
            left-hand side, while an rvalue appeared on the right-hand side:
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_ch9_lvalue_rvalue_assignment"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
int score = 90;
score = 95;
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            Modern C++ uses a more useful meaning. An lvalue identifies a
            persistent object or storage location. An rvalue is usually a
            temporary value or computation result. The expression remains an
            lvalue even when it appears on the right side of an assignment:
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_ch9_lvalue_rvalue_codelens"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20int%20score%20%3D%2090%3B%0A%20%20%20%20int%20copy%20%3D%20score%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20score%20%3D%20score%20%2B%205%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22copy%3D%22%20%3C%3C%20copy%20%3C%3C%20%22%2C%20score%3D%22%20%3C%3C%20score%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=400&amp;codeDivWidth=350&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=1&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="16:10">
        </interactive>

        <p>
            Here, <c>score</c> is an lvalue because it identifies an object,
            while <c>score + 5</c> is an rvalue because it is a temporary
            computation result. This is why the following is valid and invalid,
            respectively:
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_ch9_lvalue_address_examples"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
int* pointer = &score;   // valid: score is an lvalue
// int* invalid = &(score + 5);  // invalid: the result is an rvalue
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            This chapter uses only the practical distinction between lvalues and
            rvalues. More detailed categories, including prvalues, xvalues, and
            glvalues, can be studied later with move semantics.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_exercise_star">
        <statement><p>What does <c>*score_pointer = 95;</c> do when <c>score_pointer</c> points to <c>score</c>?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>It changes the pointed-to <c>score</c> object to 95.</p></statement><feedback><p>Dereferencing accesses the object identified by the pointer.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It changes the pointer's address to 95.</p></statement><feedback><p>The dereference operator accesses the pointed-to object, not the pointer's stored address.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It creates a second pointer.</p></statement><feedback><p>A declaration, not this assignment, creates a pointer variable.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It prints the address of <c>score</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>Output requires <c>std::cout</c>; this statement performs assignment.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_parsons_dereference" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the statements to store an address and then change the pointed-to object.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>int score = 90;</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>int* score_pointer = &amp;score;</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; *score_pointer &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>*score_pointer = 95;</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; score &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_exercise_address_of">
        <statement><p>What does <c>&amp;score</c> produce in an expression?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>The address of <c>score</c></p></statement><feedback><p>The address-of operator produces a pointer value identifying the object.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The value stored in <c>score</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Writing <c>score</c> alone reads its value; <c>&amp;score</c> asks for its address.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The object identified by <c>score</c></p></statement><feedback><p>The expression produces an address, not the object itself.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The address of the pointer variable itself</p></statement><feedback><p><c>&amp;score</c> identifies <c>score</c>, not another pointer variable.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_exercise_declaration_star">
        <statement><p>In <c>int* pointer</c>, what role does <c>*</c> play?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>It declares <c>pointer</c> as a pointer to an <c>int</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>Here the star is part of the pointer declaration syntax.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It immediately accesses the pointed-to object.</p></statement><feedback><p>Dereferencing occurs in an expression such as <c>*pointer</c> after the pointer exists.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It obtains the address of <c>pointer</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>The address-of operator is <c>&amp;</c>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It releases dynamic storage.</p></statement><feedback><p>Storage release uses <c>delete</c> or <c>delete[]</c>.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_exercise_lvalue">
        <statement><p>Which expression is an lvalue because it identifies a persistent object?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>score</c></p></statement><feedback><p>The variable name identifies a storage location and can appear on the left side of assignment.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>score + 5</c></p></statement><feedback><p>This computation produces a temporary rvalue.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>90 + 5</c></p></statement><feedback><p>A numeric computation result is an rvalue.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>5</c></p></statement><feedback><p>A literal is a value, not a named storage location.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>
</section>
